\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}
\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}

\title{Catalyst Deactivation in a Trickle-Bed Reactor:\\A Lumped Kinetic Study}
\author{G.~Ferreira and T.~Aalto}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\input{notation}
\input{chapters/kinetics}

\end{document}

\section*{Notation}
\begin{itemize}
\item[$\eta$] effectiveness factor of the catalyst pellet
\item[$\phi$] Thiele modulus
\item[$\tau$] space time, s
\item[$\varepsilon$] bed void fraction
\item[$C_A$] concentration of reactant A, mol/m$^3$
\item[$k$] intrinsic rate constant, m$^3$/(mol s)
\end{itemize}

\section{Kinetic Model}
The intrinsic kinetics follow a Langmuir--Hinshelwood form,
with the rate of disappearance of A given by
\begin{equation}
-r_A = \frac{k C_A}{1 + K_A C_A}.
\end{equation}

Pore diffusion is important for the pellet sizes used here.
The observed rate is expressed through the Thiele modulus $\eta$
and the effectiveness factor $\phi$,
so that $r_{obs} = \phi \, (-r_A)$ with
\begin{equation}
\eta = L \sqrt{\frac{k}{D_e}}, \qquad
\phi = \frac{\tanh \eta}{\eta}.
\end{equation}

Deactivation is modelled as a separable activity factor
that decays exponentially with time on stream.
